{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"length","index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We have a problem: count binary words of length 13 that do not contain any forbidden substring \"000\" or \"111\". So we need number of binary strings length 13 avoiding runs of 3 consecutive same bits. This is a combinatorics problem. We can model as counting sequences over alphabet {0,1} with no 000 or 111 as substring. Equivalent to sequences where maximum run length of identical bits is 2. So we can count using recurrence or automaton.\n\nLet a_n be number of valid strings of length n ending with a run of length 1 of some bit? Actually we need to track last two bits maybe. Let's define states based on last two bits: we need to know last two bits to ensure we don't create a forbidden run of 3. But we also need to know if last run length is 1 or 2. Equivalent to state: last bit value and run length (1 or 2). So we can have 4 states: (0,1), (0,2), (1,1), (1,2). Transition: from state (b,1) we can append same bit b to get run length 2 -> state (b,2). Or append opposite bit 1-b to get run length 1 -> state (1-b,1). From state (b,2) we cannot append same bit because would create run length 3 forbidden. So only append opposite bit -> state (1-b,1). So we can build recurrence.\n\nLet A_n = number of strings length n ending with run length 1 of 0. B_n = ending with run length 2 of 0. C_n = ending with run length 1 of 1. D_n = ending with run length 2 of 1.\n\nBase n=1: strings of length 1: \"0\" -> run length 1 of 0: A_1=1. \"1\" -> C_1=1. B_1=0, D_1=0.\n\nRecurrence for n>=2:\nA_{n} = from previous state where last bit is 1 and run length 1 or 2? Wait A_n is ending with 0 run length 1. To get that, previous string length n-1 must end with 1 run length 1 or 2, and we append 0. So A_n = C_{n-1} + D_{n-1}. Similarly, C_n = A_{n-1} + B_{n-1}.\n\nB_n: ending with 0 run length 2. To get that, previous string length n-1 must end with 0 run length 1, and we append 0. So B_n = A_{n-1}. Similarly, D_n = C_{n-1}.\n\nThus we can compute iteratively.\n\nLet's compute up to n=13.\n\nInitialize:\nn=1: A=1, B=0, C=1, D=0.\n\nn=2:\nA2 = C1 + D1 = 1+0=1\nC2 = A1 + B1 = 1+0=1\nB2 = A1 =1\nD2 = C1 =1\nSo totals: A=1,B=1,C=1,D=1. Sum=4. Indeed all 4 strings of length 2 are valid.\n\nn=3:\nA3 = C2 + D2 =1+1=2\nC3 = A2 + B2 =1+1=2\nB3 = A2 =1\nD3 = C2 =1\nSum=2+2+"}}],"created":1790979462,"model":"C:\\Users\\d2j3\\Documents\\Codex\\2026-09-10\\napraw\\outputs\\moon-native\\models\\gpt-oss-20b-MXFP4.gguf","system_fingerprint":"b10809-5266f24da","object":"chat.completion","usage":{"completion_tokens":768,"prompt_tokens":3713,"total_tokens":4481,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0}},"id":"chatcmpl-0RJw1gBXStHtkPPmi7ZlJs1lEKjTHZvy","timings":{"cache_n":0,"prompt_n":3713,"prompt_ms":451.331,"prompt_per_token_ms":0.12155426878534878,"prompt_per_second":8226.77812957674,"predicted_n":768,"predicted_ms":2288.102,"predicted_per_token_ms":2.9831838331160365,"predicted_per_second":335.21232882100537}}